---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Studio — SlushTec"
  description="About SlushTec: indie game studio in Tucson, Arizona building adult interactive worlds with permanent consequences. VARP lineage and design differentiation."
>
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-12 sm:py-16">
    <p class="text-warm-earth text-sm font-medium tracking-widest uppercase mb-3">
      Tucson, Arizona
    </p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold text-golden-glow mb-6">Studio</h1>
    
    <div class="space-y-6 text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        SlushTec builds adult interactive worlds where free-form action writes durable state,
        relationships are bidirectional, and the world keeps score. Not chatbots with furniture —
        living reactive spaces.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our core systems are the <strong class="text-white">VARP</strong> engine
        (Virtual Action Role Play) and the <strong class="text-white">True Emotion Engine</strong>.
        NPCs form bias and residue the way people do, not as forgetful transcripts.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-golden-glow pt-4">What sets us apart</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm text-left border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-surface text-gray-400">
              <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Others often ship</th>
              <th class="py-2 font-medium text-golden-glow">SlushTec ships</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-gray-300">
            <tr class="border-b border-surface/60"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Chat with a character</td><td class="py-2.5"><strong class="text-white">Act</strong> inside a reactive mini-universe</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-surface/60"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Soft reset / reload the bad choice</td><td class="py-2.5"><strong class="text-white">Permanent ledger</strong> — fail forward, no paid undo</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-surface/60"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">One-way affection that only climbs</td><td class="py-2.5"><strong class="text-white">Bidirectional</strong> trust and intimacy that can collapse</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-surface/60"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Generic helpful NPC voice</td><td class="py-2.5"><strong class="text-white">World-pack authenticity</strong> — realm rules, not customer service</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-surface/60"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Scripted trees or forgetful sessions</td><td class="py-2.5"><strong class="text-white">VARP + True Emotion Engine</strong> — bias, residue, durable state</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-surface/60"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Cloud-only dependency</td><td class="py-2.5"><strong class="text-white">Local-first</strong> (optional cloud for art / Avatar Lab)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Franchise skin over a chatbot</td><td class="py-2.5"><strong class="text-white">Original IP</strong> world packs on a shared engine</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-golden-glow pt-6">The VARP lineage</h2>
      <p>
        Live Action Role Play asked for fifty years whether you could step into the story instead of only describing it.
        Persistent digital worlds (MUDs → Ultima Online → EverQuest → World of Warcraft) proved people would treat a digital place as a second life.
        Language models finally supplied reactive agents. What almost nobody did was put the pieces back together:
        free-form action + permanent score + world law + agents that form bias instead of forgetting.
      </p>
      <p>
        VARP is that line. The chat window was a detour — not the destination.
      </p>

      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 pt-6 border-t border-surface">
        Founded by Brian “Slush” Slusher. Studio name and domain locked 2026.
        Previous working name Sonoran Forge was retired due to a local business name collision.
        Built under a hard sun.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
